item.text:
I searched German parliamentary records, federal audit material, Ukraine-recipient records, independent delivery datasets/reporting, Mali mission records, and Saudi-export reporting. The strongest recoveries are: documented early delivery shortfalls followed by verified heavy-weapons delivery; Ukraine end-use assurances and recipient tracking, but no demonstrated German on-site post-shipment inspection or misconduct remedy; suspension of German/EU training benefiting formed Malian units; significant special-fund planning defects, partial correction, and zero 2022 disbursement; and evidence that Scholz personally approved Saudi joint-program licences despite recipient-risk concerns.

Unresolved: I found no reliable weapon-specific finding that German-supplied arms caused civilian harm in 2022, no substantiated Ukrainian recipient-misconduct case involving German materiel, no German remedial action responding to such misconduct, and no public German tracing of EUTM trainees to the documented Malian abuses. The April industry-list dispute remains allegation-level, although independent delivery data resolves the broader performance question: Germany initially had a substantial commitment-delivery gap, then delivered its first heavy weapons in June. Treat WD001–WD020—not reused E001/E002—as the authoritative prior IDs.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"The Ukraine Support Tracker: Which countries help Ukraine and how?","publisher":"Kiel Institute for the World Economy","publication_date":"2022-08-01","url":"https://epicenter.bg/upfiles/KWP_2218.pdf","claim":"Independent commitment-versus-delivery tracking found that, through 7 June 2022, Germany had delivered listed light weapons and armoured vehicles but had not yet delivered its pledged Gepard systems, IRIS-T system, or MARS II launchers; the researchers identified Germany as one of the donors with a considerable gap between committed and delivered in-kind military aid.","locator":"Table 5, pp. 27-28; section 7, pp. 31-32, especially PDF lines 1027-1040 and 1141-1154.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-001","canonical_fact_key":"germany_ukraine_early_2022_commitment_delivery_gap","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.6","2B.9"],"source_type":"independent_research_dataset","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"An independent research institute systematically distinguished commitments from deliveries and supplied dated, weapon-level tables and methodological sourcing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct measurement through 7 June 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level delivery performance under Scholz; operational responsibility was shared with the defence and economic ministries, and the source does not attribute each delay personally to Scholz.","contrary_evidence":"Later 2022 evidence shows that several heavy systems were subsequently delivered, so this establishes an early shortfall rather than a year-long failure.","underlying_fact_key":"germany_ukraine_early_2022_commitment_delivery_gap","lenses":["2B.4","2B.6","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Heavy weapons from Germany arrive in Ukraine","publisher":"Deutsche Welle","publication_date":"2022-06-21","url":"https://amp.dw.com/en/first-heavy-weapons-from-germany-arrive-in-ukraine/a-62211051","claim":"Ukraine's defence minister confirmed receipt of the first German heavy weapons, seven Panzerhaubitze 2000 howitzers, on 21 June; Germany simultaneously published a list separating delivered items from outstanding pledges, including 30 Gepards and three MARS II launchers still awaiting delivery.","locator":"Paragraphs under “Panzerhaubitze 2000 'finally' part of Ukrainian arsenal” and “What else has Germany sent?”, web lines 96-121.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-002","canonical_fact_key":"germany_ukraine_pzh2000_delivery_june_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.6","2B.9"],"source_type":"independent_news_with_recipient_confirmation","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous independent reporting identifies the recipient official, delivery date, quantity, and the government's newly published delivered-versus-promised list.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly concerns delivery on 21 June 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Delivery was an action of the Scholz government, but procurement, transport, and training implementation belonged primarily to responsible ministries and partner governments.","contrary_evidence":"This corrects any inference from the early-June delivery gap that Germany never delivered heavy weapons, while confirming that notable pledges remained outstanding on 21 June.","underlying_fact_key":"germany_ukraine_pzh2000_delivery_june_2022","lenses":["2B.4","2B.6","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Opening statement during questions to the Federal Government about arms shipments to Ukraine","publisher":"Federal Foreign Office of Germany","publication_date":"2022-04-27","url":"https://www.auswaertiges-amt.de/en/newsroom/news/2524258-2524258","claim":"Foreign Minister Baerbock publicly described specific weapons only after stating that they had been safely delivered, and said the government had created an industry procurement list after exhausting readily available federal stocks; this documents the government's security-based disclosure practice and the responsible minister's account, but does not independently resolve which systems were removed from earlier lists.","locator":"Opening statement, paragraphs beginning “For that reason, now that the weapons have been safely delivered” and “In addition, when we had no more stocks of our own”.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-003","canonical_fact_key":"germany_ukraine_delivery_disclosure_april_2022","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.6","2B.9"],"source_type":"official_parliamentary_statement","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Primary evidence of the government's disclosure policy and public representation, but not independent verification of the disputed industry-list editing.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Statement delivered on 27 April 2022.","ruler_attribution":"A cabinet minister spoke for the Scholz government; the statement does not establish Scholz's personal handling of individual list entries.","contrary_evidence":"TIME and the Guardian reported that the Chancellery had offered inconsistent explanations and that an earlier industry list was reduced; this official statement cannot itself disprove those reports.","underlying_fact_key":"germany_ukraine_delivery_communication_2022","lenses":["2B.6","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Weiterverbreitung von Waffen im Kontext des Ukraine-Kriegs","publisher":"Deutscher Bundestag","publication_date":"2022-08-04","url":"https://www.bundestag.de/presse/hib/kurzmeldungen-905466","claim":"The government said it could not assess the probability of uncontrolled weapons proliferation, relied on Ukrainian end-use assurances prohibiting re-export or transfer, documented each delivery through the defence and economic ministries, and supported creation of a Ukrainian firearms-registration database.","locator":"Bundestag parliamentary-news summary, web lines 733-740.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-004","canonical_fact_key":"germany_ukraine_end_use_controls_august_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"official_parliamentary_answer","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary legislative record precisely states both the controls used and the government's admitted inability to quantify diversion risk.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Contemporaneous August 2022 answer about wartime deliveries.","ruler_attribution":"Controls and documentation were implemented by ministries within the Scholz government; no personal Scholz intervention or inspection is established.","contrary_evidence":"The record demonstrates paperwork and planned recipient-side tracing, not German physical post-shipment inspection, verified end use, or a response to proven misconduct.","underlying_fact_key":"germany_ukraine_end_use_controls_2022","lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Schriftliche Fragen mit den in der Woche vom 6. Juni 2022 eingegangenen Antworten der Bundesregierung","publisher":"Deutscher Bundestag","publication_date":"2022-06-10","url":"https://dserver.bundestag.de/btd/20/021/2002170.pdf","claim":"The government said Ukrainian end-use assurances were obtained before export or, in wartime emergencies, accepted afterward; after checking sources available to it, it had no information that German-delivered weapons or ammunition had reached criminal, terrorist, or violent extremist groups.","locator":"Question 36 and answer, pp. 31-32, PDF lines 1438-1463.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-005","canonical_fact_key":"germany_ukraine_no_known_diversion_june_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.5"],"source_type":"official_parliamentary_answer","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary, dated parliamentary response clearly separates the assurances collected from the narrower negative finding based on sources available to government.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct assessment as of 8 June 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Finding and controls belonged to the Scholz government collectively, particularly the economic and defence portfolios; it is not evidence of Scholz personally conducting monitoring.","contrary_evidence":"Absence of information is not proof of verified end use; some assurances were accepted only after shipment, and the answer reports no on-site German inspection.","underlying_fact_key":"germany_ukraine_end_use_controls_2022","lenses":["2B.4","2B.5"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"O. Danilov: Each unit of weapons provided to Ukraine by partners is under close control","publisher":"National Security and Defense Council of Ukraine","publication_date":"2022-07-21","url":"https://www.rnbo.gov.ua/en/Diialnist/5608.html","claim":"Ukraine's National Security and Defense Council presented a SOTA-system module, operational from 24 February 2022, that tracked partner weapons by donor and shipment status—including ready for shipment, in transit, and received—and said Ukrainian military institutions were being trained to use it.","locator":"Opening paragraphs and description of the SOTA weapons-supply monitoring module dated 21 July 2022.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-006","canonical_fact_key":"ukraine_recipient_weapons_tracking_sota_2022","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.5"],"source_type":"recipient_government_record","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Primary evidence that the recipient created and used a tracking mechanism, but its performance and completeness were not independently audited here.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"The system was reported operational during 2022 and specifically dated to the invasion period.","ruler_attribution":"Recipient-side implementation; it cannot be attributed to Scholz except as context for risks surrounding his government's transfers.","contrary_evidence":"This does not establish German access to the system, German post-shipment inspection, battlefield end use, or the absence of diversion.","underlying_fact_key":"ukraine_recipient_weapons_tracking_sota_2022","lenses":["2B.4","2B.5"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Anpassung bei EUTM Mali nach Militärputsch 2020","publisher":"Deutscher Bundestag","publication_date":"2022-05-19","url":"https://www.bundestag.de/presse/hib/kurzmeldungen-895744","claim":"Following the EU decision of 5 April 2022, Germany reduced its EUTM Mali contingent to a minimal presence and suspended training and tactical advice benefiting formed Malian armed-forces and National Guard units; only military-policy-level guidance and exchange continued.","locator":"Bundestag summary of government answer 20/1798, web lines 735-739.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-007","canonical_fact_key":"germany_eutm_mali_training_suspension_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"official_parliamentary_answer","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary parliamentary record specifies the date, affected beneficiaries, operational change, and residual activity.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct April-May 2022 policy and implementation evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Implemented by the German contingent and responsible ministry under the Scholz cabinet pursuant to an EU decision; it was not a unilateral Scholz action.","contrary_evidence":"The source does not identify which of roughly 16,000 historical trainees committed abuses, nor establish a German investigation, victim remedy, or weapons-control response.","underlying_fact_key":"germany_eutm_mali_training_suspension_2022","lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Sondervermögen Bundeswehr: Besser, aber weiterhin mit Risiken","publisher":"Bundesrechnungshof","publication_date":"2022-11-11","url":"https://www.bundesrechnungshof.de/SharedDocs/Kurzmeldungen/DE/-2022-Kurzmeldungen/-11-2022-sondervermoegen-bundeswehr/11-2022-sondervermoegen-bundeswehr.html","claim":"Germany's supreme audit institution found major defects in the special fund's draft economic plan: planned project spending exceeded the €100 billion ceiling, debt-service and inflation or interest buffers were omitted, and project expenditures were placed in non-binding, opaque collective titles rather than transparently assigned.","locator":"Section “Entwurf des Wirtschaftsplans mit erheblichen Mängeln”, three bullet points; report summary dated 11 November 2022.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-008","canonical_fact_key":"bundeswehr_special_fund_audit_defects_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.8","2B.9"],"source_type":"supreme_audit_institution_finding","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative independent public audit findings with specific financial-control defects and a stable section locator.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Audit reports issued on 7 October and summarized on 11 November 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The defective plan was prepared by Scholz's Defence Ministry and submitted within his government's signature initiative; operational drafting responsibility was ministerial, not shown as Scholz's personal work.","contrary_evidence":"The ministry subsequently revised the plan and adopted some recommendations, so the initial defects were not wholly left unaddressed.","underlying_fact_key":"bundeswehr_special_fund_audit_defects_2022","lenses":["2B.8","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Sondervermögen Bundeswehr: Besser, aber weiterhin mit Risiken","publisher":"Bundesrechnungshof","publication_date":"2022-11-11","url":"https://www.bundesrechnungshof.de/SharedDocs/Kurzmeldungen/DE/-2022-Kurzmeldungen/-11-2022-sondervermoegen-bundeswehr/11-2022-sondervermoegen-bundeswehr.html","claim":"After the October audit, the Defence Ministry revised the special-fund plan, reduced the projects financed through it, and sought to remain within the statutory ceiling; nevertheless, the auditor found continuing overplanning risks from procurement-cost inflation, development costs, and interest rates and recommended mandatory reporting to the Budget Committee.","locator":"Section “Risiken auch im überarbeiteten Entwurf” and concluding recommendation for a BMVg reporting duty.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-009","canonical_fact_key":"bundeswehr_special_fund_partial_correction_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.8","2B.9"],"source_type":"supreme_audit_institution_finding","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The independent auditor records both the ministry's concrete correction and the residual control risks, avoiding an announcement-only inference.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Correction and follow-up finding occurred in October-November 2022.","ruler_attribution":"A corrective response by the Defence Ministry within the Scholz government; no source establishes that Scholz personally ordered it.","contrary_evidence":"The revision was a meaningful response, but it did not eliminate the auditor's concerns and provides no evidence of equipment delivery to troops in 2022.","underlying_fact_key":"bundeswehr_special_fund_partial_correction_2022","lenses":["2B.8","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Högl: Noch kein Cent des Sondervermögens bei der Truppe angekommen","publisher":"Deutscher Bundestag / Parliamentary Commissioner for the Armed Forces","publication_date":"2023-03-14","url":"https://www.bundestag.de/dokumente/textarchiv/2023/kw11-interview-wehrbeauftragte-937590","claim":"The Parliamentary Commissioner for the Armed Forces reported that none of the €100 billion special fund had reached the armed forces during 2022 and characterized procurement as excessively slow, while calling for accelerated procurement reform.","locator":"Interview accompanying Annual Report 2022, web lines 735-740.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-010","canonical_fact_key":"bundeswehr_special_fund_zero_2022_disbursement","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.8","2B.9"],"source_type":"independent_parliamentary_oversight","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The constitutionally independent parliamentary commissioner made a precise retrospective finding tied to her 2022 annual report.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in 2023 but explicitly establishes the absence of special-fund disbursement during calendar year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome of the Scholz government's signature defence-fund implementation, with procurement responsibility distributed across the Defence Ministry, procurement administration, cabinet, and legislature.","contrary_evidence":"Some major orders were initiated in 2022 and planning was revised, but neither fact constitutes money or equipment reaching troops that year.","underlying_fact_key":"bundeswehr_special_fund_zero_2022_disbursement","lenses":["2B.8","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Report: Germany OKs arms sales to Saudi Arabia","publisher":"Deutsche Welle","publication_date":"2022-09-29","url":"https://amp.dw.com/en/german-government-approves-arms-exports-to-saudi-arabia-reports/a-63288334","claim":"Based on Economy Minister Habeck's letter to the Bundestag, independent reporting said Scholz approved Saudi export licences before his Gulf trip, including approximately €36 million in Eurofighter and Tornado equipment and ammunition and €2.8 million in A330 MRTT spare parts, all connected to multinational programmes.","locator":"Sections “New export licenses” and “Banned in 2018”, web lines 96-116.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-011","canonical_fact_key":"scholz_saudi_joint_program_export_approvals_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"independent_news_based_on_ministerial_letter","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting identifies the decision-maker, timing, equipment classes, values, and underlying parliamentary communication, although the letter itself was not recovered in an accessible stable copy.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Approvals and reporting occurred in September 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct: the ministerial letter reportedly stated that Scholz approved the licences before his trip; implementation remained shared with the export-control authorities and cabinet ministries.","contrary_evidence":"The approvals were exceptions tied to inherited multinational programmes, not proof that Germany abandoned every Saudi restriction; recipient end use and subsequent delivery are not established here.","underlying_fact_key":"scholz_saudi_joint_program_export_approvals_2022","lenses":["2B.5","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Our arms deliveries are protecting human lives","publisher":"Federal Foreign Office of Germany","publication_date":"2022-10-16","url":"https://www.auswaertiges-amt.de/en/newsroom/news/interview-baerbock-sz-2558140","claim":"Baerbock acknowledged that the government chose not to block German components for inherited European joint programmes reaching Saudi Arabia, citing reliability toward British and Spanish partners; she said the government intended future common criteria and exit clauses where human-rights standards were not met.","locator":"Interview question and answer on Saudi Arabia, web lines 141-145.","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-012","canonical_fact_key":"scholz_government_saudi_export_rationale_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"official_ministerial_interview","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary explanation by the responsible foreign minister explicitly identifies the cabinet's decision, its multinational constraint, and proposed safeguard response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Contemporaneous October 2022 explanation of the 2022 decision.","ruler_attribution":"Cabinet-level rationale under Scholz; it corroborates shared governmental ownership but does not replace the separate evidence that Scholz approved specific licences.","contrary_evidence":"The promised future criteria and exit clauses were prospective and do not establish recipient-risk review, post-shipment monitoring, or an enforceable remedy for the 2022 approvals.","underlying_fact_key":"scholz_saudi_joint_program_export_approvals_2022","lenses":["2B.5","2B.9"]}
